# Benign corpus - pinned manifest (id,version), one package per line.
#
# WHAT THIS IS: a set of widely-used, ordinary NuGet packages whose label is "overwhelmingly not
# malware" by virtue of their download counts. Scanning them tells us which capabilities are so
# ubiquitous in benign code that they carry no gating signal. It is NOT a malware corpus and it is
# NOT proof of benignity - see ADR-0021: benign by assumption is not benign by proof.
#
# WHY VERSIONS ARE PINNED: the harness's first acceptance criterion is that two runs of the same
# manifest produce byte-identical output. A floating version would silently change the numbers a
# risk-scoring ADR is calibrated against. Changing a pin changes the manifest hash recorded in the
# report, which is how a reader tells two measurements apart.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored. Order is significant: --take N keeps the
# first N entries, so append rather than insert if you want older runs to stay comparable.

